    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nThe help window has two useful properties:\n1. If you quit other buffers while it's open, nvim still exits, rather than making the help window fullscreen.\n2. if you try to quit an unsaved buffer, it doesn't hide that buffer and go fullscreen on its own.  The exit is blocked and both windows stay in place.\n\nWhat I want is to be able to create \"help\"-like windows, a la `buftype=help`, but without the problems of setting a buffer `buftype=help`.\n\nAIUI this comes down to a simple \"last window\" counting problem, where the help buffer doesn't contribute to the count, so its presence doesn't block the \"save before exit\" and \"exit\" conditions.\n\nHere are the steps to see the awkward behaviour:\n1. Start with `nvim junk.txt`, and edit the file but do not save it.\n2. Try to issue `:q` .\n    - Normally you'll be stopped from leaving because you have an unsaved file.\n3. Issue `:copen`\n4. Switch back to `junk.txt`\n5. Try `:q` again.\n    - This time the `junk.txt` window is hidden and the quickfix window goes fullscreen.  \n6. `:q` from quickfix.\n    - Now exit is interrupted and you're switched back to the unsaved `junk.txt`.\n\nContrast that behaviour with `:help` instead of `:copen`.  When you open a help window and then try to quit `junk.txt` you get the warning immediately and the help window survives.  When you save and quit, the help window is thrown out rather than going fullscreen.\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe magical property of `buftype=help` where it does not interfere with last-window semantics should be available to other buffers; but without the single-instance restriction, and without being overwritten by `:help`.\n\n",
    "closed_at": null,
